@node Introduction
@chapter Introduction
@cindex foxtrot

foxtrot is an engine for the Secure Shell protocol (SSH-2), written in C
for the GNU system.  It is the SSH counterpart of quickstep, which does the
same for QUIC and TLS, and is meant to be the substrate on which SSH and
SFTP clients and servers are built.

It is designed in the same way.  A thin cryptographic layer over
libgcrypt; incremental parsers that deliver semantic events; reactive state
machines that never block and never own a socket.  The library does no I/O.
The caller reads bytes from its transport and hands them to
@code{gf_conn_receive}, and the connection calls back with the bytes to
write.  Time is a caller-supplied clock in microseconds.  Because nothing is
hidden in the library, the same code runs under any event loop, in tests
with a virtual clock and in-memory pipes, and in programs that cannot have a
thread or a signal handler.

It is also deliberately incomplete.  Like quickstep it refuses what is
known to be insecure.  The aim is a complete, conformant, secure-by-design
implementation rather than one that is interoperable with every legacy
system.  There is no SSH-1, no CBC or CTR cipher, no separate MAC, no
finite-field Diffie-Hellman, no SHA-1 and no @samp{ssh-rsa}; a peer that can
speak nothing else is refused, and the chapter @ref{Security policy} says
why for each.

